1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Industrialization has made us more productive and made our machines more dangerous.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
As a result, the spaces where we test our latest inventions and our weapons of war

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
have grown proportionately in complexity and size.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
The Alaskan wilderness.

5
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
An unforgiving landscape carved eons ago by glaciers.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,360
Out here, the average winter temperature is at chilly zero degrees Fahrenheit, and 50 below is not uncommon.

7
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:43,880
One would think that humans, like many of their animal counterparts in this environment,

8
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:48,400
would bar the windows, slam the doors, and hibernate until the thaw.

9
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:52,520
And in a fashion, that's what most ordinary people do,

10
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:56,620
when faced with temperatures that readily freeze, antifreeze.

11
00:00:56,621 --> 00:01:00,440
ordinary people stay out of the cold

12
00:01:00,441 --> 00:01:09,560
but there's nothing ordinary about the soldiers and civilians at Fort Greeley, Alaska

13
00:01:09,561 --> 00:01:13,380
this is the home of the CRTC

14
00:01:13,381 --> 00:01:15,800
the cold regions testing center

15
00:01:15,801 --> 00:01:19,220
the premier cold weather proving grounds in the United States

16
00:01:19,221 --> 00:01:23,460
when the temperature sinks to 30 below

17
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:26,180
and often not until it gets that cold

18
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:28,140
these people have a job to do.

19
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:32,220
We generally tend to look for about a six-hour window for testing.

20
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:34,240
That gives us time to set up, conduct the test,

21
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:36,100
and take it down where we can keep a stable temperature.

22
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:41,520
If we're, say, looking for a temperature of minus 20, minus 25, minus 30, minus 40,

23
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:43,840
even or even colder down in the minus 50 degree range,

24
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:48,280
this area of Alaska has at least ten times the amount of those six-hour windows

25
00:01:48,281 --> 00:01:51,420
in that degree area of any place else you could test in North America.

26
00:01:51,421 --> 00:02:00,540
Established in 1942, Fort Greeley operated as an Army Air Corps base until 1947, when

27
00:02:00,541 --> 00:02:07,080
the War Department chose the area as the site for the first post-war cold weather maneuver.

28
00:02:07,081 --> 00:02:10,740
The Army realized they had a lot of problems because they ran into significant difficulty

29
00:02:10,741 --> 00:02:17,249
working in the cold environment. Our equipment didn't function quite properly.

30
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:18,000
We didn't

31
00:02:18,001 --> 00:02:22,000
We didn't really know how to fight in it because we hadn't really spent a lot of time training or testing in it.

32
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,093
With each ensuing decade, U.S.

33
00:02:26,094 --> 00:02:30,000
weaponry has grown more sophisticated and more powerful.

34
00:02:30,001 --> 00:02:37,000
And the wide open spaces at the Cold Regions Test Center have proven capable of accommodating this changing arsenal.

35
00:02:37,001 --> 00:02:42,000
We have around 670,000 acres here in Fort Greeley that we can use,

36
00:02:42,001 --> 00:02:47,000
which gives us the ability here to do an awful lot of firing exercises for artillery.

37
00:02:47,001 --> 00:02:49,000
We have extended ranges.

38
00:02:49,001 --> 00:02:52,000
If we combine some of the other ranges here that are nearby in Alaska,

39
00:02:52,001 --> 00:02:55,000
we can actually fire between 80 and 100 kilometers,

40
00:02:55,001 --> 00:02:58,000
which for firing rockets or firing artillery,

41
00:02:58,001 --> 00:03:01,000
the new long-range artillery everybody's developing is very good.

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Nothing is too big or too small to be evaluated in the cold weather.

43
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:14,000
We have courses designed for testing soldier equipment, boots and clothes.

44
00:03:14,001 --> 00:03:18,320
We actually even have a glove obstacle course to run gloves through an obstacle course to test how they do,

45
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:21,580
put them through the paces for wear and stuff in the cold.

46
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:26,020
But why do we need a natural environment to test in?

47
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:32,020
Wouldn't cold chambers, refrigerated rooms that can be found at most proving grounds do the job?

48
00:03:33,420 --> 00:03:38,360
In a cold chamber, it's like, think of a giant, giant freezer, like the size of a building or a room.

49
00:03:38,361 --> 00:03:45,940
Well, if you're using a piece of equipment in there, psychologically part of it is you know you can push the button and get warm and it's 75 outside the door.

50
00:03:46,360 --> 00:03:49,640
When you're up here and it doesn't work right, it might be two or three days.

51
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:53,700
And there's a whole different impact on how people use something.

52
00:03:55,780 --> 00:04:00,200
This is God's big, giant coal chamber that we're standing in right now.

53
00:04:00,201 --> 00:04:11,860
And in this cold chamber, you have the synergistic effects of all of the snow and the wind-blown dust and the wind-blown snow, the temperature variations, the shock,

54
00:04:11,861 --> 00:04:15,040
all of those things that happen in real life.

55
00:04:16,760 --> 00:04:26,400
And in God's giant cold chamber, design oversights have a way of becoming painfully obvious, as testers discovered after leaving a vehicle out in the cold.

56
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:28,680
You couldn't turn the vehicle.

57
00:04:28,681 --> 00:04:33,840
Well, it had always been tested in a cold chamber and they pulled it in and pulled it straight back out.

58
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:36,080
Well, the steering fluid froze solid on it.

59
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:42,180
You couldn't turn it, but nobody had ever discovered that because they'd never tried to turn it in the cold.

60
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:47,820
Other cold weather issues, like the interaction of hot gases in cold weather,

61
00:04:48,220 --> 00:04:51,500
only become apparent when firing live rounds in the environment.

62
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,420
Initially, what they found out from some of the helicopter-based rockets

63
00:04:55,421 --> 00:04:59,120
was you have the plume you see from a rocket when it leaves the helicopter.

64
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:01,340
Well, in the cold, that's exaggerated.

65
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:03,700
You get much, you see that it's a much bigger plume.

66
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:06,780
And what happens is the pilots couldn't see where the missile was going,

67
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:07,960
so they couldn't track it anymore.

68
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:10,820
So they either tactically had to change how they fought,

69
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:14,580
which was fly the helicopter up higher to see through the exhaust from the missile,

70
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:17,800
or change the composition of the rocket motor,

71
00:05:17,860 --> 00:05:20,500
which is what they finally did in the next version of the rockets.

72
00:05:20,501 --> 00:05:27,620
It's not just machines that are affected by exposure to the cold.

73
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:32,000
By doing sustained work outside in sub-zero conditions,

74
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:38,240
the technicians at CRTC are able to study the crucial interface between machine and operator.

75
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:45,000
We figure it normally takes about four times longer for a soldier outside in a cold environment

76
00:05:45,001 --> 00:05:48,960
to do a task as it would take him to do it in a normal, say, temperate environment

77
00:05:48,961 --> 00:05:51,120
when it's about 70, 65, 70 degrees.

78
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:53,160
Due to the bulkiness of his clothes,

79
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:57,220
the limitations due to the fact that he's got either heavy gloves or mittens on,

80
00:05:57,640 --> 00:05:59,780
or you go to push a button and your finger is so wide

81
00:05:59,781 --> 00:06:02,040
due to the clothing you're wearing to keep warm

82
00:06:02,041 --> 00:06:04,600
that you can't hit that button or flip that switch

83
00:06:04,601 --> 00:06:05,900
without flipping the one next to it.

84
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:09,720
And a lot of those things are fairly simple,

85
00:06:09,721 --> 00:06:12,520
but until you actually get out and do it in the environment, you don't know it.

86
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:18,120
The cold also teaches surprising lessons about human nature.

87
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:23,180
The more comfortable you make the soldier, the more poorly he seems to perform.

88
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:30,160
Soldiers like to get out and get with it and be dirty and cold and tough.

89
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:36,840
And we have a very lovely facility here where we can house 75 people in great comfort.

90
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:41,660
And unfortunately, some of our soldiers have called it a prison

91
00:06:41,661 --> 00:06:44,620
because they feel that they're entombed here.

92
00:06:44,621 --> 00:06:57,620
Anyone who has driven a car in icy conditions can understand why the staff at CRTC conduct exhaustive vehicle testing out on Fort Greeley's frozen tundra.

93
00:06:57,621 --> 00:07:05,620
The tracked vehicles have tremendous control problems sliding off the road, particularly if you have any speed on them.

94
00:07:05,621 --> 00:07:06,399
on them.

95
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:13,900
It was a great eye-opener for me to see a tank sliding across ice for as much

96
00:07:13,901 --> 00:07:16,120
as a quarter mile trying to stop.

97
00:07:16,121 --> 00:07:20,340
The tracks slipped on the ice. They didn't grip. And over about a two winter period,

98
00:07:20,341 --> 00:07:26,380
we worked with the program manager and the contractor to develop a special track shoe,

99
00:07:26,381 --> 00:07:29,800
and actually two special track shoes before we were done, to help it improve its grip

100
00:07:29,801 --> 00:07:33,860
so that the tank treads gripped and it moved through the snow and ice very easily.

101
00:07:33,861 --> 00:07:40,260
But a lesson learned at the Cold Regions Testing Center is not always a lesson learned.

102
00:07:40,261 --> 00:07:47,620
It is amazing to me how many times we've had to prove that water freezes at 32 degrees

103
00:07:47,621 --> 00:07:48,620
Fahrenheit.

104
00:07:48,621 --> 00:07:54,460
The thing that the developers often forget is what happens if the motor or the engine

105
00:07:54,461 --> 00:07:58,620
on this device stops for just a few minutes.

106
00:07:58,621 --> 00:08:02,280
If it does, this whole system freezes and bursts.

107
00:08:02,281 --> 00:08:06,460
It's very interesting trying to get the antifreeze in a broken system.

108
00:08:06,461 --> 00:08:10,720
You can take 90 weight axle grease, put it down to 50 below zero and drive nails with

109
00:08:10,721 --> 00:08:11,720
it.

110
00:08:11,721 --> 00:08:13,620
That's how hard it gets.

111
00:08:13,621 --> 00:08:18,840
And those things have all been learned over the years and unlearned and relearned and

112
00:08:18,841 --> 00:08:24,580
unlearned by the operating military.

113
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:30,080
As a result, much of the job of a proving ground is to work patiently with the developer

114
00:08:30,081 --> 00:08:34,320
to function as an improving ground.

115
00:08:34,321 --> 00:08:40,340
The role of any proving ground is not just to provide a report card and tell the developer

116
00:08:40,341 --> 00:08:42,540
your system passes or fails.

117
00:08:42,541 --> 00:08:48,300
The role of any proving ground is to help the developer and help the people that have

118
00:08:48,301 --> 00:08:54,500
to design and field the equipment to find out what's wrong and find out what's right.

119
00:08:54,501 --> 00:08:58,540
And then correct the things that are wrong and keep the things that are right.

120
00:08:58,541 --> 00:09:04,420
No matter how good our equipment is, that sustained operation in the cold is a killer.

121
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,780
You can really have problems. Mother Nature does not forgive.

122
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:11,220
She takes her due.

123
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:17,240
Before cold weather proving grounds were even a glimmer in a general's eye,

124
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:20,200
we had to figure out how to test weaponry.

125
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:24,440
Coming up, how to test a cannon and live to tell about it.

126
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:34,420
The military has always understood the urgency of proving weapons before putting them in the hands of soldiers in the field.

127
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:42,740
As industrialization stepped up the potency of weapons, there was an increasing need to test them systematically.

128
00:09:44,380 --> 00:09:50,600
As early as the Civil War, the military established a proving ground in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania,

129
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:54,580
to prove cannon manufactured at the nearby Fort Pitt foundry.

130
00:09:54,581 --> 00:10:00,700
It opened in July 1861 and became known as Bangtown on the Allegheny.

131
00:10:00,701 --> 00:10:07,820
In order to prove Navy cannons, testers devised a structure to control the gun's recoil.

132
00:10:07,821 --> 00:10:14,820
The structure consisted of a triangle formed with heavy timbers from which hung iron restraints

133
00:10:14,821 --> 00:10:19,420
that held the muzzle and breach of the gun in a horizontal position during firing.

134
00:10:19,421 --> 00:10:24,720
a bomb-proof shelter was built for gunners to retreat into

135
00:10:24,721 --> 00:10:27,940
before each discharge in case the cannon

136
00:10:27,941 --> 00:10:33,300
misfired and exploded guns were trained on a 10 by 12 foot target of dirt

137
00:10:33,301 --> 00:10:36,560
reinforced with logs in this way

138
00:10:36,561 --> 00:10:41,520
cannonballs could be recovered and reused this innovation greatly reduced

139
00:10:41,521 --> 00:10:42,740
the cost of ammunition

140
00:10:42,741 --> 00:10:47,400
and made repeated firing possible to test durability

141
00:10:47,401 --> 00:10:50,520
the cannons were discharged using two powder strengths.

142
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:54,840
Excessive charges, powder loads of greater than customary size,

143
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:59,240
and service charges, gunpowder loads that would normally be used in combat.

144
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:02,740
The overall aim of this proving was simple,

145
00:11:03,180 --> 00:11:06,480
to decrease the risk of malfunction and resultant casualties.

146
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:13,360
1908 marked the beginning of mass production of a new product with deadly capabilities.

147
00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:16,300
The automobile.

148
00:11:17,400 --> 00:11:29,180
As millions of drivers took to the roads, highway fatalities tripled, going from 10,000 in 1918 to more than 30,000 in 1929.

149
00:11:31,220 --> 00:11:38,460
Automakers soon found that testing on public streets and highways was neither safe for the public nor intensive enough for their needs.

150
00:11:39,020 --> 00:11:42,200
They responded by building their own test sites.

151
00:11:42,201 --> 00:11:49,300
The Packard Proving Ground in Shelby Township, Michigan, was one of the most extensive proving grounds of the period.

152
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:58,380
Its mission, a quality car every time, was achieved by engineers and rugged test drivers like Carl Alts.

153
00:11:59,240 --> 00:12:06,100
When we tested a car, the engineers listened to what we talked about, what we didn't like or what we broke down.

154
00:12:06,260 --> 00:12:10,360
We would try to break them or find fault with them.

155
00:12:10,361 --> 00:12:15,360
As they engineered it, we tried to ruin it.

156
00:12:15,361 --> 00:12:21,360
In a car market that was increasingly saturated by cookie cutter assembly line product,

157
00:12:21,361 --> 00:12:26,360
the Packard stood out as a distinctive car made by a different kind of company.

158
00:12:26,361 --> 00:12:33,360
Well, the Packard Motor Car Company, from its inception really in 1899 forward, was a special car.

159
00:12:33,361 --> 00:12:38,360
It was said to be a car for gentlemen, built by gentlemen in the early days,

160
00:12:38,361 --> 00:12:45,540
And they prided themselves on the quality and the engineering, the construction, the fit and finish.

161
00:12:46,320 --> 00:13:00,320
Back in the early 30s, the assultant in India, a Maharaja, sent his wife's pink silk slipper to Packard Motor

162
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:06,560
and said he wanted his limousine trimmed in this material in the back.

163
00:13:06,561 --> 00:13:09,440
and Packard said certainly no problem.

164
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:15,980
At the Packard Proving Grounds, attention to detail was also the order of the day.

165
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:19,360
Active from 1928 through 1956,

166
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:22,760
the grounds occupied roughly 500 acres

167
00:13:22,761 --> 00:13:25,860
and contained a 2.5-mile oval test track,

168
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,260
gravel and paved roads, a water course,

169
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:31,780
and sand traps, among other obstacles.

170
00:13:32,780 --> 00:13:36,260
We made bad roads just to test the cars.

171
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:46,560
We had roads for testing bodies, chassis, and we had sand pits for transmissions and axles.

172
00:13:46,561 --> 00:13:53,560
We had just about every road you could think of that they had even had today.

173
00:13:53,561 --> 00:13:57,560
In fact, the roads were as bad as the highways we've got in Michigan today,

174
00:13:57,561 --> 00:14:03,560
which isn't saying much for the highway department.

175
00:14:03,561 --> 00:14:09,360
But the star of the Proving Grounds was the oval test track, where cars were driven 24

176
00:14:09,361 --> 00:14:14,120
hours a day for 10,000 miles at a stretch to test endurance.

177
00:14:14,121 --> 00:14:18,880
The track was engineered so that drivers could take the turns at 100 miles per hour without

178
00:14:18,881 --> 00:14:20,480
slowing down.

179
00:14:20,481 --> 00:14:24,560
The steep banks enabled them to release the wheel and allow the car's momentum to carry

180
00:14:24,561 --> 00:14:26,360
them through the curves.

181
00:14:26,361 --> 00:14:28,680
This feature was often used to impress visitors.

182
00:14:28,681 --> 00:14:32,180
Two or three dealers would come in from Texas or someplace.

183
00:14:32,181 --> 00:14:37,180
They would put these fellas with a test driver and they would be in the back seat usually.

184
00:14:37,181 --> 00:14:43,580
And then the test driver, after he got up to speed and they were clicking along about

185
00:14:43,581 --> 00:14:49,780
100 or 105 or so, they would just get into the turn and the driver would put a cigarette

186
00:14:49,781 --> 00:14:54,780
in his mouth and turn around to the back seat and ask somebody for a match.

187
00:14:54,781 --> 00:14:58,120
well, these fellas in the back would just about have a fit.

188
00:14:58,440 --> 00:15:02,220
The war years were particularly hard on the Packard Proving Grounds.

189
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:05,840
It was used by the military to test armored vehicles.

190
00:15:06,560 --> 00:15:10,420
The track during World War II took a lot of well-intended abuse,

191
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:14,300
and that included not just the track but the off areas as well.

192
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:19,480
The hill climb area, the water bath, all of that was used to test heavy and light tanks,

193
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:23,980
self-propelled howitzers, and other types of military vehicles.

194
00:15:23,981 --> 00:15:26,880
and we tore the proving grounds up.

195
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:29,520
They had to rebuild the whole track after the war.

196
00:15:30,140 --> 00:15:33,880
We run the dirt roads and the sand all blew away

197
00:15:33,881 --> 00:15:36,140
and they had to haul back in sand to fill in

198
00:15:36,141 --> 00:15:40,300
where we made holes and tore up the tracks.

199
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:45,580
After the war, the company never really returned to its former glory

200
00:15:45,581 --> 00:15:47,400
and closed in 1956.

201
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:51,620
In the final days at the proving ground,

202
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:54,840
Donald MacArthur and his father, a Packard engineer,

203
00:15:55,300 --> 00:15:59,320
took the 1957 prototype car out for a spin on the test track.

204
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:02,640
The design car would never be built.

205
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:08,220
So we took it out on the track, and I hammered it around about 100 miles an hour,

206
00:16:08,221 --> 00:16:10,900
and there was nobody to say don't.

207
00:16:11,760 --> 00:16:15,680
And then we put it back in the garage and went home.

208
00:16:15,681 --> 00:16:21,800
but it was sad to think that this car that really would have been a nice car

209
00:16:21,801 --> 00:16:25,980
and a pretty big improvement over the earlier model.

210
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:33,140
It's too bad to think that that never was seen by the American public,

211
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:36,860
but that was the end at that point.

212
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:42,600
Today, Packard enthusiasts are attempting to convince the Proving Ground's new owners,

213
00:16:42,601 --> 00:16:48,920
the Ford Motor Company, to preserve a 13-acre portion of the site that is rich in automotive history.

214
00:16:51,720 --> 00:16:56,060
It's not too often in this day and age that a major corporation like Ford

215
00:16:56,061 --> 00:17:00,220
would have a hand in preserving a company that was a former competitor,

216
00:17:00,440 --> 00:17:02,600
but I think they know that they're presented with a unique opportunity,

217
00:17:03,460 --> 00:17:09,720
just like automotive historians are, with preserving a historic testing facility

218
00:17:09,721 --> 00:17:12,820
in its original location

219
00:17:12,821 --> 00:17:16,140
and returning it to the grandeur it once enjoyed.

220
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:24,000
Next, a proving ground that's bigger than the state of Rhode Island

221
00:17:24,001 --> 00:17:26,160
and hotter than Houston in July.

222
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:32,300
In 1928, Leon Duray broke the land speed record with a Packard engine.

223
00:17:32,301 --> 00:17:37,440
He drove an average of 124 miles per hour for a 500-mile race.

224
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:40,540
Proving Grounds will return on Modern Marvels.

225
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:45,440
We now return to Proving Grounds on Modern Marvels.

226
00:17:48,660 --> 00:17:53,840
Throughout history, the desert has been revered as a locus of spiritual power.

227
00:17:55,200 --> 00:17:56,420
A source of solitude.

228
00:17:57,700 --> 00:17:59,220
Of communion with oneself.

229
00:18:00,320 --> 00:18:03,320
Perhaps even a place to seek out a higher power.

230
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:14,440
Not these days, and not in this desert.

231
00:18:14,441 --> 00:18:20,040
This Arizona terrain that the Apaches once roamed is now the Army's Desert Environmental

232
00:18:20,041 --> 00:18:25,600
Testing Center, the Yuma Proving Ground.

233
00:18:25,601 --> 00:18:31,880
Over 1,300 square miles in size, Yuma Proving Ground has the capacity to test nearly everything

234
00:18:31,881 --> 00:18:38,880
in the US military's combat arsenal, from tanks to mines to munitions.

235
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:44,800
Yuma Proving Ground began operations during the Second World War, primarily as a test

236
00:18:44,801 --> 00:18:50,760
center for bridging and engineering equipment, which was tested on the Colorado River.

237
00:18:50,761 --> 00:18:55,440
Thousands of troops trained in the area before going to North Africa or one of the other

238
00:18:55,441 --> 00:18:58,680
active fronts.

239
00:18:58,681 --> 00:19:03,680
After the war, the area was developed as a testing center, a proving ground.

240
00:19:03,681 --> 00:19:08,680
What they needed was they needed a place to test tires, tanks, artillery.

241
00:19:08,681 --> 00:19:11,680
With the space out here, it was excellent for artillery firing.

242
00:19:11,681 --> 00:19:15,680
In 1949, they started putting this together, and at that time,

243
00:19:15,681 --> 00:19:22,680
they built 52 buildings on this location for a cost of $1,055,500.

244
00:19:22,681 --> 00:19:25,680
The site was well chosen.

245
00:19:25,681 --> 00:19:32,340
To this day, the Proving Grounds' ruggedness and size have kept area real estate development from interfering with its testing mission.

246
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:43,220
The land mass at Yuma Proving Ground is so large that we can fire weapons here safely that cannot be fired in most other places,

247
00:19:43,740 --> 00:19:51,000
particularly in the east where there's a lot of encroachment right to the very edge of the Proving Grounds there.

248
00:19:51,001 --> 00:19:53,475
Here we have almost no encroachment.

249
00:19:53,476 --> 00:20:03,580
The land mass is large enough so that we can fire a Hellfire missile in almost any direction and be certain that it won't hurt anything or anybody.

250
00:20:05,460 --> 00:20:09,020
The main challenge the desert presents is heat.

251
00:20:09,021 --> 00:20:20,859
Here on the paved dynamometer course, technician Mike Hosko ensures that every vehicle the military buys passes rigorous strength and durability tests in the intense

252
00:20:20,860 --> 00:20:21,720
desert heat.

253
00:20:21,721 --> 00:20:27,820
One of the specs that they give all the vehicle manufacturers in general

254
00:20:27,821 --> 00:20:35,440
is that every vehicle must operate at full throttle in 120-degree weather,

255
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:39,420
climbing a slope of 60%.

256
00:20:39,421 --> 00:20:44,560
And the coolant must stay at 220 degrees or cooler.

257
00:20:45,980 --> 00:20:49,640
In order to replicate the constant strain of a steep grade,

258
00:20:50,340 --> 00:20:55,560
technicians connect the five-ton tactical vehicle that they want to test to a dynamometer,

259
00:20:56,220 --> 00:21:00,020
a massive testing vehicle, which is equipped with heavy-duty brakes,

260
00:21:00,520 --> 00:21:02,620
similar to those used by the trucking industry.

261
00:21:04,640 --> 00:21:08,400
The tactical vehicle then tows the dynamometer around the course,

262
00:21:09,080 --> 00:21:11,660
straining against the constantly applied braking power.

263
00:21:12,940 --> 00:21:17,040
Picture, if you will, your own family vehicle.

264
00:21:17,041 --> 00:21:22,100
put your little Datsun in, pick an 18-wheeler out in some parking lot,

265
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:24,780
and say, hey, I want to tow you around town all day.

266
00:21:25,160 --> 00:21:30,020
That's basically the same type of strain that we put on our test vehicles out here.

267
00:21:30,780 --> 00:21:31,680
You would heat up.

268
00:21:32,920 --> 00:21:38,760
What we do to all of our test vehicles is much like when a doctor hooks you up

269
00:21:38,761 --> 00:21:43,340
to monitor your heart conditions, your blood pressure, your temperature.

270
00:21:43,760 --> 00:21:45,400
We do the same thing to a truck.

271
00:21:45,401 --> 00:21:52,820
We hook up enough wires and thermometers that we can monitor just the living functions of a truck.

272
00:21:54,140 --> 00:22:04,440
Full load cooling, which is what this vehicle conducts, is 95% sheer boredom and 5% sheer terror.

273
00:22:04,960 --> 00:22:06,840
Because for most of the time, nothing happens.

274
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:09,240
When it does happen, it happens fast.

275
00:22:09,241 --> 00:22:16,240
The test vehicles can catch fire, they can snap drive lines.

276
00:22:16,241 --> 00:22:22,240
Failing the full load cooling test is by no means the end of the line for a vehicle at the proving ground.

277
00:22:22,241 --> 00:22:26,240
There are some vehicles that come out here and they fail.

278
00:22:26,241 --> 00:22:29,240
Well, that just doesn't mean they get shipped home.

279
00:22:29,241 --> 00:22:38,240
The vehicle will actually stay here, the tech reps will come, and they'll actually come up with the fix here while they're still hooked up to us.

280
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:44,240
A weapon that combines boredom and terror in equal measure is the landmine.

281
00:22:48,240 --> 00:22:53,240
Mines were put into wide use during World War II as anti-tank devices.

282
00:22:53,241 --> 00:23:01,240
But by the 1970s, randomly targeted and remotely delivered anti-personnel mines were an accepted tactic.

283
00:23:01,241 --> 00:23:08,340
Today, these so-called dumb mines, which can detonate years after burial, injure or kill

284
00:23:08,341 --> 00:23:12,680
one victim every 20 minutes.

285
00:23:12,681 --> 00:23:19,640
The U.S. currently manufactures only smart mines, which have limited lifespans.

286
00:23:19,641 --> 00:23:25,060
Unlike other forms of ordnance, mines are particularly deadly, because once armed, they

287
00:23:25,061 --> 00:23:27,380
cannot be handled.

288
00:23:27,381 --> 00:23:32,820
As a result, Ron Jasper and his team have designed special test platforms to arm and

289
00:23:32,821 --> 00:23:35,380
deploy the mines in a controlled setting.

290
00:23:35,381 --> 00:23:45,280
We would place this ordnance into the test fixture, insert it down, slide the ordnance

291
00:23:45,281 --> 00:23:46,880
back.

292
00:23:46,881 --> 00:23:50,580
This little plate up here now holds this test plate in place.

293
00:23:50,581 --> 00:23:53,260
We can now pull the safety pin out.

294
00:23:53,261 --> 00:23:55,580
Our safety is now held in place.

295
00:23:55,581 --> 00:23:57,320
We close the door.

296
00:23:57,321 --> 00:24:01,760
Once we're ready to do the test, we send the signal that causes the mine to initiate the

297
00:24:01,761 --> 00:24:03,280
arming process.

298
00:24:03,281 --> 00:24:09,940
The mine is then propelled out this tube and into the open field for test purposes.

299
00:24:09,941 --> 00:24:13,220
We then take this unit back on the vehicle.

300
00:24:13,221 --> 00:24:16,960
We pick up the next piece of ordnance.

301
00:24:16,961 --> 00:24:22,140
This entire facility is cleaned 100% after each test.

302
00:24:22,141 --> 00:24:24,560
We use robotic vehicles.

303
00:24:24,561 --> 00:24:29,360
We pick up the ordinance that is not expended or has not detonated.

304
00:24:29,361 --> 00:24:32,740
We can then do several things with this ordinance from these remote vehicles.

305
00:24:32,741 --> 00:24:37,260
We can detonate the ordinance and blow it up to clear the range, or we can carry it

306
00:24:37,261 --> 00:24:41,840
back to another facility where we can cut this ordinance open.

307
00:24:41,841 --> 00:24:46,340
Once the ordinance is cut open remotely, we can then disassemble the ordinance and then

308
00:24:46,341 --> 00:24:49,260
do failure analysis on the ordinance.

309
00:24:49,261 --> 00:24:54,460
We look at the ordinance from its birth to its death.

310
00:24:54,461 --> 00:24:57,360
We try to expose this ordinance to the worst possible environment,

311
00:24:58,620 --> 00:25:02,400
assuming that if the ordinance performs in the worst possible environment,

312
00:25:02,520 --> 00:25:04,520
it will perform in the best possible environment.

313
00:25:05,220 --> 00:25:09,980
I feel the soldier really gains a great benefit from the testing that's conducted here.

314
00:25:10,060 --> 00:25:14,180
They know for a fact that we live up to our motto, which is test for the best.

315
00:25:14,840 --> 00:25:18,880
The equipment they'll use out on the battlefield has thoroughly been tested.

316
00:25:18,881 --> 00:25:26,600
It has been taken through more than they would actually go through during the normal tour of duty in any type of environment.

317
00:25:27,680 --> 00:25:32,760
It may look like Arizona, but it drives just like the Persian Gulf.

318
00:25:33,780 --> 00:25:39,480
Next, how the veteran testers at Yuma fought the Gulf War before the first shot was fired.

319
00:25:41,060 --> 00:25:47,280
Each year at Yuma Proving Ground, over 1 million test man hours are devoted to proving military equipment.

320
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:50,700
Proving Grounds will return on Modern Marvels.

321
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:57,520
We now return to Proving Grounds on Modern Marvels.

322
00:26:02,940 --> 00:26:08,080
In February 1991, the ground offensive of the Gulf War began.

323
00:26:09,340 --> 00:26:10,040
The U.S.

324
00:26:10,041 --> 00:26:14,000
military was uncertain of the capabilities of its battered foe, the Iraqi Army.

325
00:26:14,001 --> 00:26:18,300
but little mystery remained about the realities of a desert war.

326
00:26:21,200 --> 00:26:24,980
Much of the demystification process had already taken place

327
00:26:24,981 --> 00:26:28,120
on the vehicle testing ranges at the Yuma Proving Grounds.

328
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:38,720
No problem was too large, or where dust was concerned, too small to prepare for.

329
00:26:38,721 --> 00:26:47,900
The M1 Abrams tank, one of the stars of the eventual ground war, was vulnerable to tiny particulates.

330
00:26:52,900 --> 00:26:58,520
Its turbine-powered engine, essentially a jet engine, guzzled huge quantities of air,

331
00:26:59,060 --> 00:27:01,900
and the Iraqi desert air was thick with dust,

332
00:27:03,300 --> 00:27:08,240
a condition that with a little effort could be duplicated at the Yuma Proving Grounds.

333
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:14,520
The dust courses that have been developed at Yuma Proving Ground each have their own consistency in dust.

334
00:27:15,080 --> 00:27:20,180
And if they don't, we will refine that dust or sand to make it what we want.

335
00:27:20,700 --> 00:27:22,040
We have that ability here.

336
00:27:22,420 --> 00:27:26,900
Sometimes it's simple as driving tracks over the dust course in front of a vehicle

337
00:27:26,901 --> 00:27:34,220
to create that dust cloud that we want to portray our vehicles in convoy going from one location to another.

338
00:27:35,400 --> 00:27:38,160
Although dust may seem like a mundane problem,

339
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:43,760
the price of failure, stalled tanks and stranded soldiers, would have been dramatic.

340
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:51,760
Today, in a typical day of testing, the M1 Abrams takes quite a beating.

341
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:59,240
We would come out here to the hills course, which is approximately four miles in one circuit.

342
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:04,240
They'll run approximately three or four circuits,

343
00:28:04,241 --> 00:28:08,740
and then they'll go to the next course, which is the level cross-country course,

344
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:13,020
and they'll run two circuits of that and then come back and then they'll leave here

345
00:28:13,021 --> 00:28:16,020
and then they'll go to the dynamometer, paved dynamometer course.

346
00:28:16,800 --> 00:28:21,640
Then they'll run several miles of pavement and they'll do that throughout the day.

347
00:28:21,740 --> 00:28:23,920
You can do that, what, two, three times a day.

348
00:28:28,120 --> 00:28:30,860
When testing a tank or any other piece of equipment,

349
00:28:31,480 --> 00:28:33,920
technicians apply basic scientific principles.

350
00:28:34,240 --> 00:28:40,680
We do multiple repetitions just as any scientific procedure,

351
00:28:40,681 --> 00:28:47,820
and we try to replicate creating only one variable as a tank running on a hill course.

352
00:28:48,540 --> 00:28:53,180
We replicate that numerous times and may try it with maybe a different track

353
00:28:53,181 --> 00:28:54,940
and the same driver all the time.

354
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:58,640
If we're trying driving techniques and trying to find out the endurance,

355
00:28:58,860 --> 00:29:02,580
what the physical fatigue effects are in the driver or a pilot,

356
00:29:02,581 --> 00:29:05,820
We may change the pilot. We'll only change one variable.

357
00:29:09,740 --> 00:29:14,580
At Yuma, all manner of armored vehicles are maintained and employed in tests,

358
00:29:15,400 --> 00:29:17,560
including outdated Soviet tanks.

359
00:29:18,520 --> 00:29:22,960
The U.S. military uses these Cold War-era tanks for target practice,

360
00:29:23,380 --> 00:29:27,000
since many of them have been sold to potential enemies, like Iraq.

361
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:32,480
It's important to fire on a target that presents a realistic signature or shape.

362
00:29:32,580 --> 00:29:35,660
to the Army's radar and infrared targeting systems.

363
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:43,000
Testing and design modifications have greatly improved the infrared systems since the Gulf War,

364
00:29:43,540 --> 00:29:46,440
but at the time, night targeting was challenging.

365
00:29:47,560 --> 00:29:51,960
One, two, three, five.

366
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:56,080
You're working with an infrared heat signature picture.

367
00:29:56,081 --> 00:29:58,380
It's not really a picture as you imagine.

368
00:29:58,540 --> 00:30:01,100
It's just the differences in heat between one object to another.

369
00:30:02,080 --> 00:30:04,680
So in a desert environment, there's not a whole lot of difference in heat.

370
00:30:05,200 --> 00:30:07,720
So we have to know which vehicles we're looking at.

371
00:30:08,160 --> 00:30:11,240
We have to know what their shapes are.

372
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:15,600
And it does become a problem of trying to identify vehicles at night.

373
00:30:15,960 --> 00:30:16,900
It's a limiting factor.

374
00:30:16,901 --> 00:30:20,320
You really are going to be hard-pressed to shoot vehicles you can't identify at night.

375
00:30:22,120 --> 00:30:26,560
Weapons and soldiers, then, are only as good as the information systems that support them.

376
00:30:26,561 --> 00:30:33,560
Battles are increasingly fought in volleys of data rather than munitions.

377
00:30:33,561 --> 00:30:40,560
The combatant who computes fastest fires first and most accurately.

378
00:30:40,561 --> 00:30:45,760
In this test, a Kiowa Warrior is receiving a software upgrade that allows it to send

379
00:30:45,761 --> 00:30:49,820
digital messages to all branches of the military.

380
00:30:49,821 --> 00:30:54,160
The Kiowa Warrior is the military's eyes and ears on the battlefield.

381
00:30:54,161 --> 00:30:58,260
primarily a reconnaissance helicopter, it must be able to communicate clearly.

382
00:30:59,260 --> 00:31:05,820
We are an observation helicopter, so we take the observations that we make,

383
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,120
put them into a digital message, send them to the artillery unit,

384
00:31:10,440 --> 00:31:13,360
and the artillery unit uses that information to act on it.

385
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:15,920
It's called joint variable message format,

386
00:31:16,600 --> 00:31:20,500
and we're testing that to go to a digital battlefield

387
00:31:20,501 --> 00:31:26,720
where we're able to send messages across the division to all users on the battlefield.

388
00:31:27,520 --> 00:31:33,880
And the situational awareness that commanders are going to have when JVMF is fully implemented

389
00:31:33,881 --> 00:31:39,640
is they'll basically be able to know where all the troops are on the division at any given time,

390
00:31:39,641 --> 00:31:43,740
and that will allow them to employ the troops much more efficiently.

391
00:31:45,220 --> 00:31:48,980
Surviving on the battlefield means fielding communication systems

392
00:31:48,981 --> 00:31:50,560
that send information quickly,

393
00:31:51,080 --> 00:31:54,700
because being heard can mean being located by the enemy.

394
00:31:54,701 --> 00:31:58,760
A short burst is less likely to be picked up by an enemy

395
00:31:58,761 --> 00:32:02,440
than the long voice messages or a long transmission

396
00:32:02,441 --> 00:32:04,380
that we may have had in the past.

397
00:32:04,940 --> 00:32:07,820
We can now send that information from the field

398
00:32:07,821 --> 00:32:11,100
to the operations center in an instance

399
00:32:11,101 --> 00:32:15,580
where before it could have taken minutes to hours.

400
00:32:15,581 --> 00:32:28,060
The battlefield now will have the tactical internet set up so that they can send these messages to each other the same way you would send email to a friend of yours.

401
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:35,500
For the men and women at the Yuma Proving Ground, the Gulf War underscored the value of rigorous testing.

402
00:32:36,540 --> 00:32:44,900
It was really a strange time because I have two kids, and as of now, you know, they're both of military age.

403
00:32:44,901 --> 00:32:58,901
So I sort of have a personal type of desire to make sure that any truck we test passes here

404
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:03,180
because my son or daughter may have to use it in the field.

405
00:33:05,820 --> 00:33:09,100
Next, expanding the limits of the human machine,

406
00:33:10,060 --> 00:33:13,580
testing the bodies and minds of America's elite athletes.

407
00:33:14,900 --> 00:33:24,140
Five miles from the nearest power line, Yuma's smart weapons testing complex gets its electricity from a solar power field.

408
00:33:25,460 --> 00:33:29,140
Proving Grounds will return on Modern Marvels.

409
00:33:32,680 --> 00:33:40,940
An athlete propels twice her body weight into the air, the force of her momentum actually lifting her off the ground for a brief moment.

410
00:33:40,941 --> 00:33:48,860
anywhere else this would be an extraordinary almost freakish occurrence a cause to stop and stare

411
00:33:48,861 --> 00:33:55,040
but here at the u.s olympic complex in colorado springs colorado

412
00:33:55,041 --> 00:34:02,660
this level of exceptional behavior is merely routine for this is a proving ground for the

413
00:34:02,661 --> 00:34:06,770
rarest of machines, world-class athletes.

414
00:34:06,771 --> 00:34:11,180
In 1997, the United States Olympic Committee

415
00:34:11,181 --> 00:34:17,640
opened its new $23 million phase 2 facility on this site, including a state-of-the-art

416
00:34:17,641 --> 00:34:22,002
sports medicine and sports science center.

417
00:34:22,003 --> 00:34:26,780
Today, the complex can house up to 557 coaches

418
00:34:26,781 --> 00:34:31,980
and athletes.

419
00:34:31,981 --> 00:34:36,300
Men and women from all over come here to train, to be treated and to be tested by

420
00:34:36,301 --> 00:34:44,920
the top coaches, doctors and sports scientists in the country.

421
00:34:44,921 --> 00:34:51,300
The complex, like any proving ground, has a dual mission.

422
00:34:51,301 --> 00:34:55,960
First, to test, and then to refine the athlete's performance.

423
00:34:56,960 --> 00:35:00,320
Big benefit here is everything is right there for you.

424
00:35:01,100 --> 00:35:03,700
You have your sports medicine, you have your sports science,

425
00:35:04,200 --> 00:35:07,820
you have your dorms are right here, you have your cafeteria right here.

426
00:35:07,821 --> 00:35:11,400
The athlete doesn't need to leave the complex.

427
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:16,340
But how strong are the athletes?

428
00:35:17,120 --> 00:35:18,600
How fit can they become?

429
00:35:18,601 --> 00:35:23,160
How do you measure and test the limits of a highly tuned human machine?

430
00:35:24,860 --> 00:35:27,300
This is the province of the sports scientist,

431
00:35:27,860 --> 00:35:32,740
who will submit the athletes to a battery of physiological and biomechanical tests

432
00:35:32,741 --> 00:35:35,000
throughout their training period of the complex.

433
00:35:36,960 --> 00:35:41,020
This system right here is basically a metabolic assessment system.

434
00:35:41,200 --> 00:35:46,700
It's a metabolic cart, and it allows us to measure aerobic capabilities of the athletes

435
00:35:46,701 --> 00:35:52,180
by assessing the concentration of oxygen and carbon dioxide that they expire

436
00:35:52,181 --> 00:35:55,660
under progressively higher exercise loads.

437
00:35:56,620 --> 00:36:01,920
During exercise, blood vessels bring oxygen to muscles and remove lactate,

438
00:36:01,921 --> 00:36:04,800
which is produced in the body's metabolic process.

439
00:36:05,820 --> 00:36:12,860
As exercise intensifies, lactate may accumulate as the system falls behind, causing fatigue.

440
00:36:13,740 --> 00:36:16,600
Trainers need to know exactly when this fatigue begins.

441
00:36:16,700 --> 00:36:26,700
The sports scientists at the Olympic Training Center also have a variety of machines that analyze athletes' technique in their respective events.

442
00:36:26,701 --> 00:36:34,700
This device that's in the center of this elevated platform here is what we call a force platform.

443
00:36:34,701 --> 00:36:42,700
This is just a platform that's designed to measure what we call ground reaction forces or the interaction between the athlete and the ground.

444
00:36:42,701 --> 00:36:45,240
we can see how power is being delivered.

445
00:36:45,840 --> 00:36:46,720
Are they symmetrical?

446
00:36:47,020 --> 00:36:49,600
Is one leg doing more work than the other?

447
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:54,040
In that case, does that have an impact on their subsequent technique?

448
00:36:55,080 --> 00:36:57,060
The obstacles are complex.

449
00:36:57,280 --> 00:37:01,340
Ability to control and sense and feel the difference in kinematics,

450
00:37:01,720 --> 00:37:05,260
in velocities, in accelerations, in force productions,

451
00:37:05,440 --> 00:37:08,120
in pressure against the floor, in dealing with the balance,

452
00:37:08,121 --> 00:37:10,500
in keeping the barbell moving on a straight vertical line

453
00:37:10,501 --> 00:37:14,580
from the floor up to 1.5 meters up overhead.

454
00:37:15,140 --> 00:37:16,080
It's pretty complex.

455
00:37:17,980 --> 00:37:22,740
The sports scientist's ability to analyze performance is almost limitless.

456
00:37:22,980 --> 00:37:26,780
What I'm showing you here are two separate lifts

457
00:37:26,781 --> 00:37:30,800
that have actually synchronized together

458
00:37:30,801 --> 00:37:34,200
so that we can actually look to see if her technique

459
00:37:34,201 --> 00:37:37,720
is staying consistent across different weights.

460
00:37:37,721 --> 00:37:45,908
So as I play this, we play it in slow motion, you can see that it appears as though there's actually two different lifters, but in this case it's actually the same

461
00:37:45,909 --> 00:37:46,609
lifter.

462
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:52,320
And you can see some very slight technical differences between the lighter and heavier weights there.

463
00:37:53,220 --> 00:37:59,160
In analyzing and testing our athletes' movements, we are looking for models of maximum efficiency.

464
00:37:59,161 --> 00:38:08,295
That's why we're doing the testing, the analysis, the feedback, and the work together in order to match as much as possible those perfect models received from the

465
00:38:08,296 --> 00:38:10,100
numbers of testing and analysis.

466
00:38:11,460 --> 00:38:17,280
With the help of technology, a motion as simple as a punch can be dissected and improved.

467
00:38:17,980 --> 00:38:25,600
What we have on the screen right now is one of our Olympic boxers actually working on the instrumented boxing bag.

468
00:38:25,601 --> 00:38:31,060
And so this bag that you see here actually has a series of accelerometers

469
00:38:31,061 --> 00:38:33,540
that have been dropped into the middle of the bag.

470
00:38:33,880 --> 00:38:39,460
So we're able then to measure the peak force in pounds of each punch,

471
00:38:39,780 --> 00:38:42,680
how many punches the athlete actually throws,

472
00:38:43,420 --> 00:38:46,680
the amount of time that that punch stays in contact with the bag.

473
00:38:46,800 --> 00:38:47,720
This is in milliseconds.

474
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:51,500
And we kind of talk about this as a punch effectiveness measurement.

475
00:38:51,501 --> 00:38:57,900
This intense amount of physiological and technical scrutiny will only partially answer the question,

476
00:38:58,460 --> 00:39:02,540
will this athlete function at maximum potential at, say, the Olympic Games?

477
00:39:03,360 --> 00:39:09,320
For the rest of the answer, you'd have to ask one of the sports psychologists who work closely with the competitors.

478
00:39:10,140 --> 00:39:16,180
If we take a look at an athlete for an example, I mean, we really are creating some amazing, incredible athletic machines

479
00:39:16,181 --> 00:39:19,620
with the technology that we have these days, but at the same time,

480
00:39:19,621 --> 00:39:24,240
And we still have to train our operators, our on-board operators, our athletes, to be

481
00:39:24,241 --> 00:39:31,060
able to run those machines that they've created in the most efficient way possible.

482
00:39:31,061 --> 00:39:35,240
There are a whole host of distractions that can interfere with an athlete's performance.

483
00:39:35,241 --> 00:39:40,560
You may be overseas and they'll get there and the food's not what they like to eat.

484
00:39:40,561 --> 00:39:47,560
The beds may not be comfortable, it's too noisy, and they get out on the platform and

485
00:39:47,561 --> 00:39:53,560
There may be 5,000 people out there, and they're not used to lifting in front of more than a couple hundred people.

486
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:59,060
I have trouble with getting real tense because I'm so anxious and excited and ready to go

487
00:39:59,061 --> 00:40:04,700
that I can't sort of just calm myself down, you know, and just take my aggressiveness out on the bar.

488
00:40:04,701 --> 00:40:07,940
You do get tense during competitions, especially after a bad event.

489
00:40:08,140 --> 00:40:13,320
Or after a great event, you are just so psyched up, you might get too psyched for the next competition

490
00:40:13,321 --> 00:40:17,200
where you have to worry about technique and actually have to slow yourself down.

491
00:40:17,560 --> 00:40:24,560
Sports psychologists attempt to simplify, to limit the athlete's focus to the task at hand.

492
00:40:24,561 --> 00:40:30,560
It's really a matter of what do I need to do, what's important for my performance right now at this moment.

493
00:40:30,561 --> 00:40:35,560
I've had people write cues on the back of their hand or on the tips of their shoes

494
00:40:35,561 --> 00:40:42,560
or maybe have a piece of tape on their bow or on their oar, you know, or on the back of their glove.

495
00:40:42,561 --> 00:40:48,480
something they see that will cue them and remind them to think about what the task is at hand here.

496
00:40:48,800 --> 00:40:50,320
You don't want to think about too many things,

497
00:40:50,680 --> 00:40:53,080
but at the same time you want to have one or two cues in each event

498
00:40:53,081 --> 00:40:56,320
to get you prepared for your throw, your jump, or your run.

499
00:40:56,560 --> 00:40:59,320
The biggest thing I do is imaging, picturing myself doing the lift

500
00:40:59,321 --> 00:41:01,760
from both the first person and the third person point of view,

501
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:05,620
and just seeing myself making them over and over again

502
00:41:05,621 --> 00:41:09,340
and hopefully then going out on the platform and doing it where it counts the right way.

503
00:41:09,341 --> 00:41:19,340
What does it say about our culture that we put so much energy, ingenuity and financial resources into improving performance at sport?

504
00:41:19,341 --> 00:41:24,140
I think this goes back to the reason why the US came to be.

505
00:41:24,141 --> 00:41:32,440
That was to be creative and to be free and be independent and come up with new ideas and do things better and do things differently.

506
00:41:33,060 --> 00:41:36,720
As we obsessively improve ourselves and our machines, however,

507
00:41:37,360 --> 00:41:40,060
there is cause to worry that balance will be lost.

508
00:41:40,820 --> 00:41:43,400
Even the machines, we turn those off at night.

509
00:41:43,620 --> 00:41:44,920
You know, we sort of give them a rest.

510
00:41:44,980 --> 00:41:48,820
They're not running 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

511
00:41:49,040 --> 00:41:52,020
So what's important for us is we get better with our technology,

512
00:41:52,021 --> 00:41:57,200
get more creative, and come up with this idea of being better all the time.

513
00:41:57,620 --> 00:42:01,520
We also take some time to make sure we've got some balance to life as well for our athletes.

514
00:42:01,521 --> 00:42:09,580
But for now, the relentless pursuit of performance continues day and night in scorching heat and freezing cold.

515
00:42:10,100 --> 00:42:13,520
Anywhere where someone asks how far, how fast.

